<h2>Seoul's Palaces & Temples — Where the Past Lives</h2>
<p>One of Seoul's most remarkable qualities is how seamlessly ancient history coexists with ultra-modern urbanism. Step out of a glass skyscraper and you're facing a 600-year-old royal palace. Wander through a Buddhist temple courtyard and you'll hear the subway rumbling beneath you. Seoul's historical sites are not museums frozen in amber — they're living, breathing places that Koreans still use, visit, and celebrate.</p>

<h2>🏯 The Five Grand Palaces of Seoul</h2>

<h3>1. Gyeongbokgung Palace (경복궁) — The Must-Visit</h3>
<p>The largest and most iconic of Seoul's five palaces. Built in 1395 as the main palace of the Joseon Dynasty, Gyeongbokgung (Palace Greatly Blessed by Heaven) dominates the northern part of the city with its imposing gates, vast courtyards, and the stunning Gyeonghoeru Pavilion reflected in its lotus pond.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Admission:</strong> ₩3,000 adults, ₩1,500 children. Free in hanbok (traditional dress).</li>
  <li><strong>Hours:</strong> 9am–6pm (5pm in winter). Closed Tuesdays.</li>
  <li><strong>Don't miss:</strong> Changing of the Royal Guard ceremony (10am and 2pm daily)</li>
  <li><strong>Tip:</strong> Wear or rent hanbok at the palace entrance — free entry and beautiful photos</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Changdeokgung Palace (창덕궁) — UNESCO World Heritage</h3>
<p>More intimate and arguably more beautiful than Gyeongbokgung. The palace is famous for its Secret Garden (Huwon) — 78 acres of forested gardens with pavilions, ponds, and ancient trees. A UNESCO World Heritage Site.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Admission:</strong> ₩3,000. Secret Garden: ₩5,000 (timed entry, book ahead)</li>
  <li><strong>Hours:</strong> 9am–5:30pm. Closed Mondays.</li>
  <li><strong>Tip:</strong> Book Secret Garden tickets online in advance — slots sell out quickly</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Deoksugung Palace (덕수궁) — City Center Oasis</h3>
<p>The only palace in central Seoul, surrounded by modern office towers. Uniquely, Deoksugung has both traditional Korean and Western-style buildings — a result of Korea's forced modernization in the early 20th century. The Stone Wall Road (Deoksugung Doldam-gil) alongside the palace is one of Seoul's most romantic walks.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Admission:</strong> ₩1,000</li>
  <li><strong>Hours:</strong> 9am–9pm. Closed Mondays.</li>
  <li><strong>Don't miss:</strong> Changing of the Guard ceremony at Daehanmun Gate (11am, 2pm, 3:30pm)</li>
</ul>

<div class="tip-box">
  💡 <strong>Hanbok tip:</strong> Renting hanbok (traditional Korean clothing) near the palace entrances costs ₩10,000–₩20,000 for 2–3 hours. You get free palace entry and the photos are spectacular, especially with the palace architecture as backdrop. Multiple rental shops near Gyeongbokgung and Changdeokgung.
</div>

<h2>🛕 Must-Visit Temples in Seoul</h2>

<h3>Jogyesa Temple (조계사) — Heart of Korean Buddhism</h3>
<p>The head temple of the Jogye Order of Korean Buddhism, located right in the center of Seoul near Insadong. Founded in 1395, the current buildings date from 1938. During Buddha's Birthday (usually May), the temple courtyard fills with thousands of colored lanterns — one of Seoul's most spectacular sights. Daily services are open to respectful visitors.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Open:</strong> 24 hours, free admission</li>
  <li><strong>Location:</strong> 5-minute walk from Insadong</li>
  <li><strong>Temple stay programs</strong> available for deeper cultural immersion</li>
</ul>

<h3>Bongeunsa Temple (봉은사) — Gangnam's Hidden Temple</h3>
<p>A functioning Buddhist temple in the heart of Gangnam, directly opposite the COEX Mall. Utterly surreal — ancient incense smoke drifting past glass towers. The giant standing Buddha statue and pagoda make for incredible photos. Temple stay programs available.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Open:</strong> 5am–10pm, free admission</li>
  <li><strong>Best time:</strong> Early morning when monks perform chanting ceremonies</li>
</ul>

<h3>Gilsangsa Temple (길상사) — The Poet's Temple</h3>
<p>Perhaps Seoul's most beautiful temple — a former high-end restaurant converted into a temple by a Buddhist poet. Set in a wooded hillside in northern Seoul. Much quieter than the central temples, with an almost meditative atmosphere.</p>

<div class="tip-box">
  💡 <strong>Temple etiquette:</strong> Remove shoes when entering temple halls. Dress modestly (no shorts or sleeveless tops). Don't photograph monks without permission. Bow slightly when entering — a sign of respect. Speaking quietly is expected. Photography of the exterior is generally fine.
</div>

<h2>🏰 City Walls & Fortresses</h2>

<h3>Seoul City Wall (한양도성)</h3>
<p>Built in 1396 to mark the boundary of the Joseon capital, 18.6km of wall still circles central Seoul. Walk sections of the wall for stunning views over the city. The Naksan and Bugaksan sections are most popular. Free to hike.</p>

<h3>Namhansanseong Fortress</h3>
<p>A UNESCO World Heritage mountain fortress 35km from Seoul. Excellent hiking with dramatic views. Takes about 1 hour by public transport. A great half-day trip.</p>

<h2>📅 Planning Your Visit</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Most palaces are closed one day a week (usually Tuesdays or Mondays)</li>
  <li>Arrive early to avoid crowds — the first hour after opening is peaceful</li>
  <li>The Seoul City Pass includes entry to all five palaces</li>
  <li>Audio guides available in English at Gyeongbokgung and Changdeokgung</li>
  <li>Combine Gyeongbokgung with Bukchon Hanok Village and Insadong for a full cultural day</li>
</ul>

